/T (\(7\) At the height of tulip mania in 1637, the market suddenly collapsed. Buyers who had been willing to pay exorbitant prices for tulips lost confidence, leading to a rapid decline in demand. Almost overnight, the once-priceless bulbs became nearly worthless, leaving many investors bankrupt. This dramatic crash, now regarded as one of the first recorded financial bubbles, demonstrated the dangers of unchecked speculation. Though the financial losses were immense, the lesson learned from tulip mania remains relevant even today.)
